# Notebook Links

Every form of link between notebooks, for checking navigation in a real VS Code
workbench. Open this file, click a link, and confirm where you land.

## Linking to another notebook

- [The whole notebook](links-target.webnb)
- [A section of it](links-target.webnb#beta)
- [A section, spelled out](<links-target.webnb#The Fetch API, really?>)
- [From the workspace root](/links-target.webnb#beta)
- [A notebook in a subfolder, with spaces in its name](<sub/links nested.webnb>)

## Linking inside this notebook

- [Down to the last section](#the-last-section)
- [Back to the top](#notebook-links)

## Linking from an output cell

The click handler covers the whole notebook webview, so a link in a rendered
output -- a multiple choice prompt, a walkthrough note -- navigates too.

```{mcq}
Which section of the target notebook does [this link](links-target.webnb#alpha) open?
correct: Right. The link names `#alpha`, so it opens Alpha.
wrong: Follow the link and see which heading lands at the top.
[x] Alpha
[ ] Beta
```

## Links that should not navigate

- [A notebook that does not exist](missing.webnb) warns that there is no such notebook.
- [A section that does not exist](links-target.webnb#no-such-section) opens the
  notebook and warns about the section.
- [An ordinary web link](https://code.visualstudio.com) opens in a browser, as always.
- [A source file](sub/notes.txt) keeps VS Code's own behavior.

## Filler

Enough prose to push the last section out of view, so a link to it has somewhere
to scroll. One paragraph is not enough on a tall screen, so here are several.

Notebooks in this course run to a few screens each, and a section link is only
worth having when the section it names starts off the screen.

A reader following a link should land with the heading at the top of the editor,
not merely somewhere in the same notebook.

If the heading is already visible, revealing it should still be harmless: the
list scrolls to put that cell at the top either way.

Which is what the last section below is for.

## The Last Section

You arrived here from a link inside this same notebook.
